        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<BR>
Eye pressure changes due to microgravity were investigated. The fluid shift to the upper body during adaptation to microgravity may cause an increase in the intraocular pressure. In the early phase after reaching microgravity there is an intraocular pressure peak. This may pose a potential danger to certain crew members who may be at risk for developing glaucoma.
<P>
APPROACH:<BR>
A hand applanation tonometer (designed for use in microgravity) was used to monitor changes in the aqueous humor and volume of the inner eye of two subjects during the mission. Measurements were also obtained pre- and postflight.
<P>
RESULTS:<BR>
During the STS-61A (D-1) mission, three payload specialists measured intraocular pressure with the hand applanation tonometer.  The first measurements, taken 1 hour after launch, revealed a mean pressure rise of 20 to 25%.  It is assumed that even these early measurements are already masked by the adaptation processes.  Measurement of the absolute peak values might have been missed, as they are expected immediately after reaching microgravity conditions. The investigators concluded that following measurements during the first two mission days should give information about an alteration of the normal diurnal rhythm observed on Earth.
<P>
To get earlier measurements in microgravity, a fully automatic self-tonometer was developed.  It functioned independent of position and microgravity. The hardware was tested during the 8 day German-Russian Mir mission in 1992.  A total of 70 measurements of intraocular pressure (IOP) were performed in the Soyuz-TM capsule, as well as in the Mir space station, using the automatic self-tonometer. The first pressure readings 16 minutes after reaching microgravity revealed a 92% rise in the intraocular pressure (IOP), compared to the baseline data on Earth.  This was followed by an adaptation phase lasting several hours. Important information about the handling of the self-tonometer in microgravity was obtained. 
<P>
During STS-55 (D-2), two German scientists and one American astronaut obtained self-tonometric pressure profiles on 6 mission days (44 pressure values determined out of 144 measurements). The initial peak values 15 minutes after entering microgravity revealed a pressure increase of 114% compared to the baseline data (average pressure: right eye 10.0 mmHg, left eye 9.9 mmHg; peak values: right eye 22.6 mmHg, left eye 22.2 mmHg). Pressure values exceeding the baseline data by more than 50% were registered up to the third mission day. As to circadian rhythm, there was no difference between inflight data and baseline data before and after the mission.
        </div>

